IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc22-verdicts.l4 -- sections 660 to 669.3 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (Part XX: verdicts: attempt and included offences, infanticide; previous convictions; proof of previous convictions; fingerprint examiners; jurisdiction on the death or appointment of a judge). Part agent ccc22. Layer 1: the law, objectively.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- How a result reads: JUST TRUE (the text's own words bring the case within the rule); JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words); NOTHING (not covered). A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Tags in the comments: [M-a] a failed "if"/"where" or a flat negative counted as the text's no; [M-b] a prohibition or grant limited by its own wording counted as NOT the text's no (NOTHING);
-- [F:...] a fork line for NOTES.md (may-if, may-where, entitled, duty, unless, greater-certainty). Section 2 terms and other parts' answers are INPUTS named for their section.

§ `Sections 660 to 661 -- Attempt and the complete offence`

-- src:13218 | 660 Where the complete commission of an offence charged is not proved but the evidence establishes an attempt to commit the offence, the accused may be convicted of the attempt.
@ref s 660 (src:13218-13218)
GIVEN `the complete commission of the offence charged is proved` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the evidence establishes an attempt to commit the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 660 the accused may be convicted of the attempt` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the complete commission of the offence charged is proved`) AND `the evidence establishes an attempt to commit the offence`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the complete commission of an offence charged is not proved but the evidence establishes an attempt to commit the offence, the accused may be convicted of the attempt" [F:may-where] [M-a]

-- src:13220 | 661 (1) Where an attempt to commit an offence is charged but the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence, the accused is not entitled to be acquitted, but the jury may convict him of the attempt unless the judge presiding at the trial, in his discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence.
@ref s 661(1) (src:13220-13220)
GIVEN `an attempt to commit an offence is charged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 661(1) the accused is entitled to be acquitted` MEANS
    IF `an attempt to commit an offence is charged` AND `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the accused is not entitled to be acquitted" [F:entitled] [M-a]
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where an attempt to commit an offence is charged but the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence"

-- src:13220 | 661 (1) Where an attempt to commit an offence is charged but the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence, the accused is not entitled to be acquitted, but the jury may convict him of the attempt unless the judge presiding at the trial, in his discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence.
@ref s 661(1) (src:13220-13220)
GIVEN `an attempt to commit an offence is charged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge presiding at the trial, in the judge’s discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 661(1) the jury may convict the accused of the attempt` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`an attempt to commit an offence is charged` AND `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an attempt to commit an offence is charged but the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence, ... the jury may convict him of the attempt" [F:may-where] [M-a]
        IF `the judge presiding at the trial, in the judge’s discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the judge presiding at the trial, in his discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence" [F:unless] [M-a]
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:13220 | 661 (1) Where an attempt to commit an offence is charged but the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence, the accused is not entitled to be acquitted, but the jury may convict him of the attempt unless the judge presiding at the trial, in his discretion, discharges the jury from giving a verdict and directs that the accused be indicted for the complete offence.
@ref s 661(1) (src:13220-13220)
GIVEN `an attempt to commit an offence is charged` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 661(1) the judge presiding at the trial may discharge the jury from giving a verdict and direct that the accused be indicted for the complete offence` MEANS
    IF `an attempt to commit an offence is charged` AND `the evidence establishes the commission of the complete offence`
